<p>The dwarf reed snake escapes predators by leaping into a continuous cartwheel that&#8217;s four times as fast as slithering.</p>
<p>&#8220;The acrobatics involved in rolling are a fascinating example of how much snakes can accomplish with their limbless bodies,” Toho University ecologist Yosuke Kojima <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/01/science/these-snakes-cartwheel-out-of-dangerous-situations.html?unlocked_article_code=1.NVE.CTVQ.doX9-uYSyomO">told the <em>New York Times</em></a>.</p>
<p>&#8220;It&#8217;s nice to be surprised by weird snake behaviour occasionally,&#8221; said herpetologist Frank Burbrink. &#8220;It is truly remarkable and probably fairly arresting for any predators that encounter these little guys.&#8221;</p>
